BEGIN;

INSERT INTO questions (section_id, topic, stem, choices, correct_index, explanation, difficulty, cognitive_level)
VALUES

-- Q1: medium / L3 — Clearly trivial threshold effect on accumulation
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Bridgeport Electronics, the engagement team set the clearly trivial threshold at $3,000. The auditor discovers a $2,500 posting error in office supplies expense. What is the most likely effect of this threshold on the auditor''s accumulation of this misstatement?', '["The auditor is not required to accumulate this misstatement because it falls below the clearly trivial threshold", "The auditor must accumulate this misstatement but may exclude it from the summary of uncorrected misstatements", "The auditor should accumulate this misstatement and communicate it to management as a significant deficiency", "The auditor must report the misstatement to those charged with governance regardless of the threshold"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under AU-C 450.05, the auditor must accumulate all misstatements identified during the audit except those that are clearly trivial. Because the $2,500 error falls below the $3,000 clearly trivial threshold, it need not be accumulated. Choice B is incorrect because misstatements below the clearly trivial threshold are excluded entirely from accumulation, not merely from the summary of uncorrected misstatements.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q2: easy / L1 — Definition of clearly trivial
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'Under AU-C 450, the term "clearly trivial" describes misstatements that are:', '["Below performance materiality but above the individually significant threshold", "Inconsequential in amount and nature, whether taken individually or in the aggregate", "Less than 5% of overall materiality as required by auditing standards", "Immaterial to the financial statements taken as a whole but still documented in workpapers"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under AU-C 450.A2, clearly trivial misstatements are those that are inconsequential whether taken individually or in the aggregate, and of a wholly different order of magnitude than materiality. The auditor need not accumulate such misstatements. Choice A is incorrect because clearly trivial is a separate, lower threshold than performance materiality and is not defined by reference to it.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q3: medium / L3 — Deficiency in design vs. deficiency in operation
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'Sterling Manufacturing implemented a three-way purchase order match for all vendor invoices. During testing, the auditor finds that while the control is properly designed, accounts payable clerks routinely bypass the matching process for invoices under $1,000. What is the most likely classification of this finding?', '["A deficiency in the design of the control because the policy does not address low-dollar invoices", "A compensating control failure because alternative procedures should have been established", "A deficiency in the operation of the control because the existing control is not operating as designed", "An inherent limitation of internal control that does not constitute a reportable deficiency"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under AU-C 265.A5, a deficiency in operation exists when a properly designed control does not operate as designed or when the person performing the control lacks the necessary authority or qualifications. The three-way match was properly designed but clerks bypass it for small invoices, making this an operating deficiency. Choice A is incorrect because the control design itself is adequate—the failure is in execution, not in the policy''s structure.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q4: easy / L1 — Communication content for uncorrected misstatements
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'Under AU-C 450, the auditor is required to communicate which of the following to management?', '["Only those uncorrected misstatements that individually exceed performance materiality", "Only those uncorrected misstatements related to accounting estimates and judgments", "All misstatements accumulated during the audit, including clearly trivial amounts", "All uncorrected misstatements accumulated during the audit and the effect they may have on the opinion"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under AU-C 450.08, the auditor must communicate all uncorrected misstatements to management and request that they be corrected. This communication includes the aggregate effect and whether uncorrected misstatements could cause the financial statements to be materially misstated. Choice C is incorrect because clearly trivial misstatements are excluded from accumulation under AU-C 450.05 and therefore are not part of this communication.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q5: medium / L2 — Iron curtain approach for evaluating misstatements
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'Cedar Ridge Corporation has a $40,000 overstatement of revenue that originated two years ago but has not been corrected. The misstatement remains on the current-year balance sheet. Overall materiality is $200,000. Using the iron curtain approach, the auditor evaluates the misstatement by:', '["Quantifying the total amount by which the current-year balance sheet is misstated regardless of when the misstatement originated", "Considering only the portion of the misstatement that originated in the current year", "Averaging the misstatement over the number of years it has been present to determine annual impact", "Excluding the misstatement if it was evaluated and accepted in a prior audit period"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under AU-C 450.A18, the iron curtain approach quantifies a misstatement based on the total amount by which the current-period balance sheet is misstated, regardless of whether the misstatement originated in the current or a prior period. Choice B is incorrect because it describes the rollover approach, which considers only the current-year originating amount and may understate the cumulative balance sheet effect.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q6: medium / L2 — Rollover approach for evaluating misstatements
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'Maple Valley Distributors has carried forward a $28,000 understatement of depreciation expense that originated three years ago. No additional depreciation errors occurred in the current year. Overall materiality is $150,000. Using the rollover approach, the auditor would evaluate the current-year income statement effect as:', '["$28,000, because the full cumulative amount must be considered in each annual evaluation", "Zero, because no new depreciation misstatement originated in the current year", "$28,000 divided by three, to allocate the impact evenly across affected years", "$28,000, adjusted for the time value of money over the three-year period"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under AU-C 450.A19, the rollover approach quantifies a misstatement based only on the amount originating in the current period. Because no new depreciation error occurred this year, the current-year rollover income statement effect is zero. Choice A is incorrect because it describes the iron curtain approach, which would include the full cumulative $28,000 balance sheet effect regardless of origination period.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q7: easy / L2 — Identifying a deficiency in design
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Pinecrest Retail, the auditor discovers that management has no established control to authorize or review new vendor additions in the accounts payable master file. Under AU-C 265, this situation is best classified as:', '["A deficiency in the operation of an existing vendor management control", "An inherent limitation that cannot be addressed through internal control", "A deficiency in the design of internal control over the vendor approval process", "A significant deficiency only if unauthorized vendors have already been added"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under AU-C 265.A3, a deficiency in design exists when a necessary control is missing or when an existing control is not properly designed to meet its objective. The absence of any control over new vendor additions represents a missing control, which is a design deficiency. Choice A is incorrect because there is no existing control that is failing to operate—the control simply does not exist.', 'easy', 2),

-- Q8: hard / L3 — IT general control deficiency cascading to dependent application controls
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Northstar Financial Group, the engagement team identifies a deficiency in IT general controls: the change management process for the ERP system lacks formal testing and approval before deploying code changes. Multiple automated application controls for revenue recognition, accounts payable, and payroll depend on the ERP system. What is the most likely effect of this IT general control deficiency on the auditor''s evaluation?', '["The deficiency affects only the specific application control that was most recently modified", "The auditor should evaluate the deficiency in isolation from the dependent application controls", "The deficiency is limited to the IT environment and does not affect financial reporting controls", "The auditor should evaluate whether the deficiency undermines reliance on all dependent automated application controls"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under AU-C 265.A8, a deficiency in IT general controls—such as inadequate change management—can undermine the effectiveness of application controls that depend on the IT environment. Because revenue, payable, and payroll controls all rely on the ERP system, the auditor must evaluate the cascading effect. Choice A is incorrect because an IT general control deficiency potentially affects all dependent application controls, not just the most recently modified one.', 'hard', 3),

-- Q9: hard / L4 — Fraud by senior management as material weakness indicator
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Lakeview Capital Partners, the engagement team discovers that the CFO intentionally reclassified $180,000 of operating expenses as capital expenditures to meet earnings targets. The misstatement is below the $500,000 overall materiality. The CFO acknowledges the reclassification and agrees to correct it. The auditor should evaluate whether this finding constitutes:', '["A material weakness in internal control, because fraud committed by senior management is a strong indicator regardless of the misstatement amount", "A significant deficiency only, because the misstatement is below overall materiality and was corrected by management", "A control deficiency that does not require communication because management voluntarily corrected the error", "A significant deficiency that should be elevated to a material weakness only if additional fraud instances are identified"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under AU-C 265.A14, identified fraud by senior management is a strong indicator of a material weakness in internal control, even if the misstatement amount is immaterial. The intentional nature of the act demonstrates a breakdown in the control environment and tone at the top. Choice B is incorrect because the dollar amount and subsequent correction do not mitigate the significance of management fraud as an indicator of material weakness.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q10: hard / L4 — Restatement as material weakness indicator
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'Ridgecrest Holdings is required to restate its prior-year financial statements due to errors in lease classification under ASC 842. The restatement was prompted by the current-year audit team''s identification of the errors, not by the company''s own internal processes. The auditor should evaluate whether this situation:', '["Is a significant deficiency because the errors originated in a prior year and were subsequently corrected through restatement", "Is a strong indicator of a material weakness because the restatement demonstrates that internal controls failed to prevent or detect a material misstatement", "Does not constitute a control deficiency because the errors related to implementation of a complex accounting standard", "Is a significant deficiency that can be downgraded if management implements corrective procedures before year-end"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under AU-C 265.A14, a restatement of previously issued financial statements to correct a material misstatement due to error is a strong indicator of a material weakness. The fact that external auditors, rather than internal controls, identified the error reinforces that internal controls failed. Choice A is incorrect because the need for a restatement indicates a control failure severe enough to be considered a material weakness indicator regardless of origination period.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q11: medium / L3 — Qualitative materiality of misstatement affecting debt covenant
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'Ashland Fabrication has a loan covenant requiring a current ratio of at least 1.5:1. The company''s current ratio before adjustments is 1.52:1. The auditor identifies a $12,000 uncorrected misstatement that would reduce current assets. Overall materiality is $300,000. What is the most likely effect of this misstatement on the auditor''s evaluation?', '["The misstatement is immaterial because $12,000 is far below the $300,000 overall materiality threshold", "The misstatement should be evaluated using performance materiality rather than overall materiality", "The misstatement may be material due to qualitative factors, as it could cause a violation of the debt covenant", "The misstatement is clearly trivial and need not be accumulated or communicated to management"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under AU-C 450.A4, the auditor considers both quantitative and qualitative factors when evaluating misstatements. A misstatement that causes a debt covenant violation is qualitatively material even if quantitatively immaterial. The $12,000 adjustment would push the current ratio below 1.5:1, triggering a default. Choice A is incorrect because materiality assessment is not limited to the dollar amount—qualitative effects such as covenant compliance must also be considered.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q12: hard / L4 — Recurring unremediated control deficiency escalation
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the prior-year audit of Westbrook Packaging, the auditor communicated a significant deficiency in the month-end bank reconciliation process. In the current year, the auditor finds the same deficiency persists—reconciliations continue to be prepared weeks late with no supervisory review. Management acknowledged the issue both years but has not remediated it. The auditor should evaluate whether:', '["The deficiency remains a significant deficiency because its classification was already determined in the prior year", "The deficiency should be communicated again but cannot be reclassified since no new control failures have occurred", "The communication is not required in the current year because the same deficiency was previously communicated", "The recurrence and lack of remediation may elevate the deficiency to a material weakness, reflecting a broader control environment failure"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under AU-C 265.A12, the auditor must consider whether previously communicated deficiencies that remain unremediated should be reassessed for severity. Persistent unremediated deficiencies combined with management''s failure to take corrective action may indicate a broader control environment weakness that elevates the finding. Choice A is incorrect because classification is not fixed—recurring unremediated deficiencies warrant reassessment of severity each period.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q13: easy / L1 — Communication of other deficiencies to management
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'Under AU-C 265, control deficiencies that are neither significant deficiencies nor material weaknesses may be communicated to:', '["Management, either in writing or orally at the auditor''s discretion", "Those charged with governance as part of the required written communication", "Regulatory authorities if the deficiencies relate to compliance controls", "The predecessor auditor as part of the succession communication process"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under AU-C 265.12, the auditor may communicate control deficiencies that are not significant deficiencies or material weaknesses to management, either in writing or orally. These lesser deficiencies do not require formal communication to those charged with governance. Choice B is incorrect because only significant deficiencies and material weaknesses must be communicated in writing to those charged with governance under AU-C 265.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q14: medium / L2 — Communication timing 60-day requirement
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'Granite Peak Consulting completed its audit of Ironwood Manufacturing on March 15, 2026. During the engagement, the audit team identified two significant deficiencies. Under AU-C 265, the written communication of these deficiencies to those charged with governance must be made no later than:', '["Before the audit report date so governance can respond before the opinion is issued", "Within 30 days of completing fieldwork to ensure timely notification", "On or before the audit report date, included as a supplement to the audit report", "60 days following the audit report release date"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under AU-C 265.13, the auditor must communicate significant deficiencies and material weaknesses in writing to those charged with governance no later than 60 days following the report release date. This allows time for the auditor to finalize evaluations while ensuring timely communication. Choice A is incorrect because the standard permits communication up to 60 days after the report release date, not before the audit report date.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q15: easy / L1 — Accumulation requirement exception
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'Under AU-C 450, the auditor is required to accumulate all misstatements identified during the audit except those that are:', '["Below performance materiality", "Related to accounting estimates", "Clearly trivial in amount and nature", "Voluntarily corrected by management"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under AU-C 450.05, the auditor must accumulate all identified misstatements during the audit, except those that are clearly trivial. Clearly trivial means the misstatements are inconsequential whether taken individually or in the aggregate. Choice A is incorrect because performance materiality is used to set the scope of audit procedures, not as a threshold for excluding misstatements from accumulation.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q16: hard / L4 — Audit committee oversight weakness as MW indicator
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Summitview Energy Corp., the auditor observes that the audit committee met only once during the fiscal year, did not review interim financial information, and lacked a member with financial expertise. Several financial reporting errors were detected only by the external audit team. The auditor should evaluate whether:', '["The audit committee''s limited activity is a control deficiency but not a material weakness indicator because the external audit ultimately caught the errors", "The finding should be classified as a significant deficiency because audit committee matters are governance issues rather than internal control matters", "The limited oversight is not a control deficiency because audit committee effectiveness falls outside the scope of the financial statement audit", "The ineffective audit committee oversight is a strong indicator of a material weakness, because the audit committee is a critical component of the control environment"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under AU-C 265.A14, ineffective oversight by those charged with governance is a strong indicator of a material weakness. The audit committee plays a critical monitoring role in the COSO control environment. Infrequent meetings, no interim review, and lack of financial expertise indicate a pervasive monitoring failure. Choice A is incorrect because the external audit is not a substitute for effective internal oversight, and reliance on auditors to catch errors itself suggests a control breakdown.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q17: medium / L3 — Effect of corrected misstatements on control assessment
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Brookdale Industries, the engagement team identifies eight misstatements totaling $95,000, all of which management corrects. Performance materiality is $75,000. Although the financial statements are now correct, the nature and magnitude of the corrected misstatements most likely affects the auditor''s assessment of:', '["The opinion type, requiring a qualified opinion to disclose the number of corrections made", "The subsequent events evaluation period, which must be extended after significant corrections", "Management''s representation letter, which must specifically list each corrected misstatement by account", "The risk of material misstatement, because the pattern of errors may indicate a control deficiency"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under AU-C 450.07, the auditor considers the implications of corrected misstatements, including whether they indicate risks of additional undetected misstatements. Eight corrections exceeding performance materiality suggest potential control weaknesses that may require revised risk assessments or additional procedures. Choice A is incorrect because corrected misstatements do not result in opinion modifications—the financial statements as revised are fairly stated.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q18: easy / L1 — Error vs. fraud distinction in misstatement classification
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'Under auditing standards, the distinction between an error and fraud in classifying misstatements is based on whether the misstatement is:', '["Intentional or unintentional", "Material or immaterial to the financial statements", "Corrected or left uncorrected by management", "Quantitative or qualitative in nature"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under AU-C 240.02, fraud involves intentional misstatements while errors are unintentional. This distinction is critical because identified fraud has different implications for the audit, including expanded communication requirements and reassessment of management integrity. Choice B is incorrect because both errors and fraud can be either material or immaterial—materiality is separate from whether the misstatement was intentional.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q19: easy / L1 — Pervasive misstatement definition
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'Under AU-C 705, a misstatement is considered pervasive to the financial statements when it:', '["Exceeds overall materiality by more than 50%", "Is not confined to specific elements and represents a substantial proportion of the financial statements", "Involves fraud committed by senior management of the entity", "Remains uncorrected after the auditor communicates it to those charged with governance"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under AU-C 705.A1, effects are pervasive when they are not confined to specific elements, accounts, or items of the financial statements; they represent a substantial proportion of the financial statements; or are fundamental to users'' understanding. Choice A is incorrect because pervasiveness is determined by the scope and spread of the misstatement across the financial statements, not solely by a quantitative threshold.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q20: medium / L3 — Net vs. gross evaluation of misstatements
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'Clearwater Logistics has uncorrected overstatements of $45,000 in accounts receivable and $38,000 in accounts payable. Overall materiality is $60,000. When evaluating the aggregate effect of these misstatements on the financial statements, the auditor should consider that:', '["The misstatements net to $7,000, which is clearly trivial and requires no further evaluation", "Each misstatement must be evaluated independently, and because both exceed 50% of materiality the opinion must be modified", "Although the misstatements partially offset on the income statement, the balance sheet remains overstated by $45,000 and $38,000 in the respective accounts", "The misstatements should be combined and compared only to performance materiality rather than overall materiality"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under AU-C 450.A16, the auditor considers that offsetting misstatements in the income statement may still result in material misstatements on the balance sheet. Although the net income effect is small, both accounts receivable and accounts payable are individually overstated, potentially misleading users about the entity''s financial position. Choice A is incorrect because netting misstatements across accounts ignores the balance sheet impact and individual line-item materiality.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q21: medium / L3 — Systematic bias indicated by pattern of uncorrected misstatements
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Highland Construction, the engagement team identifies six uncorrected misstatements, each between $3,000 and $7,000, totaling $29,000 against overall materiality of $100,000. All six misstatements involve overstatement of revenue by recording deposits for future work as current-period revenue. The auditor''s evaluation should give particular attention to:', '["Whether the aggregate amount exceeds performance materiality, as this is the sole criterion for evaluating uncorrected misstatements", "The quantitative immateriality of each individual misstatement, which supports concluding the financial statements are fairly stated", "Whether management provided a written representation that the misstatements are immaterial, which resolves the matter", "The common nature and direction of the misstatements, which may indicate a systematic bias in management''s revenue recognition"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under AU-C 450.A3, the auditor considers whether uncorrected misstatements share common characteristics—such as nature, cause, or direction—that could indicate a systematic pattern. Six overstatements all involving premature revenue recognition suggest management bias, even though the aggregate is below materiality. Choice A is incorrect because the evaluation is not limited to a quantitative comparison—the pattern, direction, and nature of misstatements are equally important qualitative considerations.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q22: medium / L2 — Prior period uncorrected misstatements carried forward
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'Birchwood Electronics had $20,000 in uncorrected misstatements from the prior-year audit related to an overstatement of prepaid expenses that reverses into the current year. When beginning the current-year audit, the auditor should:', '["Include the prior-year uncorrected misstatements in the current-year summary and evaluate their effect on the current period", "Exclude the prior-year misstatements because they were already evaluated and accepted in the prior period", "Require management to restate the prior-year financial statements before beginning current-year fieldwork", "Communicate the prior-year misstatements to the incoming engagement partner only if there was a change in auditors"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under AU-C 450.06, the auditor must include in the current-year evaluation the effect of prior-period uncorrected misstatements on the current-period financial statements. The $20,000 prepaid overstatement that reverses would understate current-year expenses, affecting the current period. Choice B is incorrect because misstatements do not disappear after being passed—their effects carry forward and must be considered in each subsequent period.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q23: easy / L1 — Documentation requirements for control deficiency evaluation
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'Under AU-C 265, the auditor is required to document:', '["Only material weaknesses that were identified and communicated during the audit", "The basis for determining whether identified control deficiencies are significant deficiencies or material weaknesses", "All deficiencies communicated to management, including those that are clearly inconsequential", "The names of specific employees responsible for each identified control deficiency"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under AU-C 265.15, the auditor must document the basis for the determination of whether identified deficiencies, individually or in combination, are significant deficiencies or material weaknesses. This documentation supports the auditor''s professional judgment. Choice A is incorrect because the documentation requirement extends to the evaluation process for all identified deficiencies, not just those ultimately classified as material weaknesses.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q24: medium / L2 — Subsequent event revealing control deficiency
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'On February 20, 2026, after the December 31, 2025 audit report date of February 10 but before the report release date of March 1, Whitfield Manufacturing discovers a $250,000 embezzlement that occurred throughout 2025. The theft was concealed by overriding segregation-of-duties controls. Under AU-C 265, the auditor should:', '["Take no action because the discovery occurred after the audit report date", "Include the embezzlement only in the subsequent events note to the financial statements", "Evaluate the control override as a potential material weakness and communicate it to those charged with governance", "Withdraw the audit report and reperform all substantive procedures before reissuing"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under AU-C 265.09, the auditor evaluates control deficiencies identified at any point during the engagement, including from subsequent events before the report release date. An embezzlement concealed by overriding segregation of duties is a strong indicator of a material weakness under AU-C 265.A14. Choice A is incorrect because the 60-day communication window extends well beyond the audit report date, and deficiencies discovered before report release must be evaluated.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q25: medium / L3 — Staff turnover as control deficiency consideration
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'Riverdale Foods experienced turnover of three of its five accounting staff members during the year. The remaining staff processed all transactions but frequently made errors in period-end accruals and account reconciliations. The engagement team identified $55,000 in aggregate misstatements, below the $200,000 materiality. What is the most likely implication for the auditor''s control deficiency evaluation?', '["The staffing changes are an operational matter and not relevant to the auditor''s evaluation of internal controls", "The misstatements are below materiality, so no control deficiency exists regardless of the staffing situation", "The auditor should recommend that management hire additional staff but need not classify the situation as a control deficiency", "The staffing shortfall is relevant to evaluating whether a control deficiency exists, because inadequate resources can impair control effectiveness"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under AU-C 265.A7, the auditor considers whether staffing levels and competence affect the ability of controls to function effectively. High turnover leading to frequent errors suggests the entity lacks sufficient qualified personnel to operate controls as designed, which may constitute a significant deficiency. Choice A is incorrect because personnel resources directly affect the operating effectiveness of internal controls and are therefore relevant to the auditor''s deficiency evaluation.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q26: medium / L2 — Misstatement affecting regulatory compliance materiality
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'Beacon Health Services receives federal grant funding with a compliance materiality threshold of $10,000 for the grant program. The auditor identifies an $8,000 misstatement in the grant expenditure account. Overall financial statement materiality is $350,000. The auditor should evaluate this misstatement by:', '["Considering both financial statement materiality and the lower compliance materiality applicable to the grant program", "Using only the $350,000 financial statement materiality, as compliance matters are outside the scope of the financial statement audit", "Reporting the misstatement directly to the federal grantor agency without further evaluation", "Deferring the evaluation entirely to the single audit engagement team"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under AU-C 450.A4, the auditor considers qualitative factors, including the effect of misstatements on compliance with regulatory requirements. The $8,000 misstatement, while far below financial statement materiality, approaches the $10,000 compliance threshold and may have consequences beyond the financial statements. Choice B is incorrect because the auditor must consider the broader context of misstatements, including regulatory implications, not just overall financial statement materiality.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q27: easy / L1 — Management representation regarding uncorrected misstatements
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'Under AU-C 450, the auditor requests a written representation from management stating that:', '["All identified misstatements were caused by unintentional errors rather than fraud", "The effects of uncorrected misstatements are immaterial, individually and in the aggregate, to the financial statements as a whole", "Management has corrected all misstatements identified during the audit", "No additional misstatements exist beyond those identified by the audit team"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under AU-C 450.14, the auditor requests written representation from management that uncorrected misstatements are immaterial, individually and in the aggregate, to the financial statements. The summary of uncorrected misstatements is typically included with or attached to the representation letter. Choice A is incorrect because the representation relates to the materiality of uncorrected misstatements, not to whether they were caused by error or fraud.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q28: hard / L4 — Component auditor MW communication in group audit
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the group audit of Pacific Rim Holdings, the component auditor of a significant subsidiary identifies a material weakness in the subsidiary''s inventory valuation controls. The subsidiary''s total assets represent 40% of consolidated assets. The group engagement team did not identify any control deficiencies at the parent level. The group engagement partner should evaluate whether:', '["The material weakness is limited to the component level and does not require communication at the group level", "The component auditor''s finding should be downgraded to a significant deficiency because group-level controls were effective", "The component-level material weakness should be evaluated for its significance to the group financial statements and communicated to group governance if appropriate", "The group engagement team must reperform the component auditor''s testing before any classification can be made at the group level"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under AU-C 600.50, the group engagement team must evaluate the significance of component-level findings to the group financial statements. A material weakness at a subsidiary representing 40% of consolidated assets likely has group-level significance and should be communicated to those charged with governance of the group. Choice A is incorrect because a material weakness at a significant component with 40% of consolidated assets cannot be dismissed as a component-only matter.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q29: medium / L2 — Antifraud program deficiency evaluation
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Copperfield Industries, the auditor discovers that the company has no whistleblower hotline, no fraud risk assessment process, and no documented code of ethics. Under AU-C 265, the absence of these antifraud program elements is most appropriately evaluated as:', '["An operational matter outside the scope of the auditor''s control deficiency evaluation", "A single deficiency in the control environment that has no direct effect on financial reporting", "A reportable condition that must be included in the auditor''s report on internal control", "A deficiency or combination of deficiencies in the control environment that may indicate a significant deficiency or material weakness"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under AU-C 265.A9, the control environment—including management''s commitment to integrity, ethical values, and antifraud measures—is foundational to effective internal control. The absence of a whistleblower hotline, fraud risk assessment, and code of ethics represents multiple deficiencies that, combined, may constitute a significant deficiency or material weakness. Choice B is incorrect because control environment deficiencies directly affect the reliability of financial reporting.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q30: medium / L2 — Materiality revision based on changed circumstances
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'Midway through the audit of Eastgate Properties, the engagement team has accumulated $140,000 in identified misstatements. The original overall materiality was $250,000 based on preliminary financial data. Updated financial statements show that total revenues declined 30% from the planning-phase estimate. The auditor should:', '["Reassess whether the original materiality level is still appropriate given the significant change in the financial statement base", "Maintain the original materiality because it was properly determined during planning and should not be changed during the audit", "Automatically reduce materiality to 70% of the original amount to match the revenue decline", "Increase the clearly trivial threshold to offset the higher volume of accumulated misstatements"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under AU-C 320.12, the auditor must revise materiality if information comes to the auditor''s attention during the audit that would have caused a different determination initially. A 30% decline in total revenues significantly reduces the applicable benchmark, potentially making the original $250,000 materiality too high. Choice B is incorrect because materiality is not fixed at planning—the auditor is required to revise it whenever circumstances change the financial statement base.', 'medium', 2)
;

COMMIT;
